            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Beginnings and Surrealist Influence</strong><br>\nHenri Cartier-Bresson was born in 1908 in Chanteloup, France. After an early interest in painting, he was drawn into the world of photography by the Surrealist movement's emphasis on chance and spontaneity, which would greatly influence his later work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Development of the Decisive Moment</strong><br>\nCartier-Bresson is perhaps best known for his concept of \"the decisive moment\"—the instant when all the elements in the photographic frame align and the emotion of the story is captured, a principle that guided his photography throughout his career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World Travels and Humanist Approach</strong><br>\nCartier-Bresson traveled extensively, photographing in Europe, the Americas, Asia, and Africa. His humanist approach focused on the everyday lives of people, reflecting a deep interest in the human condition and a profound respect for his subjects.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
