            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Philosophy and Retirement</strong><br>\nCartier-Bresson retired from photography in the early 1970s to return to drawing and painting. He believed that his photographic career was a mere \"slice of time\" in his artistic life, underscoring his broader vision of art beyond a single medium.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nHenri Cartier-Bresson passed away in 2004, leaving behind a profound legacy as a pioneer in the art of photography. His work has inspired countless photographers and remains vital for its embodiment of the human experience.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enduring Influence on Photography</strong><br>\nCartier-Bresson's influence on photography is immeasurable. His insistence on capturing life as it happens laid the groundwork for modern photojournalism and has continued to shape the practices and philosophies of photographers worldwide.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
